          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Richard Wagner's Early Life</strong><br>\nRichard Wagner was born in Leipzig, Germany, in 1813. His interest in music was cultivated early on, though he didn't receive formal music education until later in life. He wrote his first play at the age of 11 and was profoundly influenced by the works of Beethoven.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Developing a Unique Style</strong><br>\nWagner's early compositions did not immediately earn him fame, but he persisted, developing a style that would challenge traditional opera. His works featured complex textures, rich harmonies, and leitmotifs—a technique associating musical themes with specific characters or ideas.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Revolutionizing Opera</strong><br>\nWagner sought to revolutionize opera with his concept of \"Gesamtkunstwerk,\" a \"total work of art\" that synergizes music, libretto, staging, and visual arts. This holistic approach transformed the expectations and experiences of theater.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
