          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Patronage of King Ludwig II</strong><br>\nKing Ludwig II of Bavaria became an important patron to Wagner, providing him with the resources to pursue his artistic vision. The king's support was crucial for Wagner to establish the Bayreuth Festival, dedicated to performing his works.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Late Works and Philosophical Writings</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Wagner wrote \"Parsifal,\" his final opera, and numerous essays and articles. His philosophical writings covered a wide range of topics, including art, politics, and society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Wagner's Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nWagner died in 1883 in Venice, Italy. His legacy endures, with his operas performed regularly around the world. He is remembered as one of the most important figures in the history of Western music for his innovations in composition and theatrical production.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
